findings:
- finding_id: I1
  title: Dependencies inline instead of workspace level
  weight: 3
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: 'Reviewer noted unused dependencies (finding #10) but did not flag inline
    version specs vs workspace = true. Dev added chrono and uuid inline in Cargo.toml.'
- finding_id: I2
  title: 'FieldErrorCode missing #[non_exhaustive]'
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: 'No phase mentioned #[non_exhaustive] on FieldErrorCode. Reviewer discussed
    core types but not this specific enum attribute.'
- finding_id: I3
  title: Hardcoded trace_id 'none'
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: true
  caught_by: tea
  evidence: TEA wrote tests expecting trace_id in the response envelope meta, but
    the test contract expects it to be derived from request context. However, reviewer
    did not explicitly flag the hardcoded 'none' value. TEA tests check trace_id exists
    but may not verify it's non-hardcoded.
- finding_id: I4
  title: Zero structured logging
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: "Reviewer finding #12: 'No structured tracing despite tracing dependency\
    \ \u2014 The tracing crate is declared but unused. No request spans, no tenant_id/trace_id\
    \ correlation, no structured logging.'"
- finding_id: I5
  title: Security comment claims tracing that doesn't exist
  weight: 3
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No phase explicitly flagged a SECURITY comment claiming logging exists
    when it doesn't. Reviewer noted absence of tracing but didn't call out misleading
    security comments specifically.
- finding_id: I6
  title: Non-ASCII tenant header silently treated as missing
  weight: 8
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No test was written for non-ASCII characters in X-Tenant-ID header. TEA's
    security_edge_cases.rs covers header injection but not non-ASCII tenant header
    silent failure.
- finding_id: I7
  title: "CWE-209 test gap \u2014 no body assertion"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: TEA wrote a CWE-209 test but the reviewer output does not indicate the
    test asserts on response body content. The ground truth confirms only status code
    is checked.
- finding_id: I8
  title: CORS preflight test doesn't verify CORS headers
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: tea
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: "Reviewer finding #7: 'CORS headers missing from non-preflight responses\
    \ \u2014 The cors_middleware only adds Access-Control-Allow-* headers to OPTIONS\
    \ responses.' This implicitly covers the issue that CORS tests only check status,\
    \ since the headers aren't even present."
- finding_id: I9
  title: "Wrong log levels \u2014 error! for 4xx client errors"
  weight: 5
  phase_ideal: reviewer
  caught: false
  caught_by: null
  evidence: No phase discussed differentiating log levels between 4xx (warn!) and
    5xx (error!). Reviewer noted absence of logging entirely but did not address log
    level granularity.
- finding_id: I10
  title: CORS allows Any origin/method/header (CWE-942)
  weight: 8
  phase_ideal: dev
  caught: true
  caught_by: reviewer
  evidence: "Reviewer finding #8: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * is overly permissive\
    \ for a security platform' \u2014 directly identifies wildcard CORS as a security\
    \ issue, though does not cite CWE-942 specifically."
